emented streaming them so people  can still worship  he also tries to keep his community  connected through bible study  weekend worship services sunday's school  class and his prayer conference call  this pandemic is larger than anything  that you've ever gone through  but god has taken you through things and  what i would encourage you to do is  remember what he's done from  for you in the past we have to  understand is the same god that created  us created scientists and created  doctors and if they found a vaccine that  could help  prevent us from experiencing the pain  and even  death from covid19 i would encourage you  to do so  bowen's message to the community don't  let the pandemic discourage you he says  quote  this too shall pass  you don't see too many black judges in  nevada courtrooms but that is slowly  starting to change  orcomanna introduces us to two local  african-american judges who have made  history  and explains how they are encouraging  the next generation  i think it's very important that the  court represent this community  trailblazer icon judge tierra jones  balances tons of titles thanks to her  historic  position of power i became the first  african-american woman to sit on the  state court bench in the state of nevada  that happened in 2017 jones tells me her  diversity brings a different perspective  to the courtroom  something extremely essential as african  americans continue to be  disproportionately incarcerated  one of the reasons i wanted to be a  judge is to make a difference it's my  job to follow the law but there's also  always  different life experiences and different  things that  are part of your culture that are a part  of your background  that you bring to your everyday  decisions there are 58 judges in  nevada's eighth judicial district court  which serves clark county  jones is one of five african americans  currently on that bench  some were just elected in 2020 i  officially started on january 4.  dee butler is the 