e the area  there's a sense of pride that we all  have we love telling people that i'm  from the west side  we wear it on our shoulder and this will  just reiterate the fact that  when they say that people will know  where the historic west side is  the sign on the north side of the u.s 95  off ramp is not completely finished yet  but will have a four foot wide walking  path allowing people to come up and take  pictures also in this neighborhood the  historic  west side school it's a two-room  schoolhouse built in 1923 with two more  rooms added in 1928.  it first served as a school for local  paiute children but as the area grew  with big construction projects  such as what would become nellis air  force base and hoover dam  it became a school to almost exclusively  african american students  during segregation the historic west  side was the only place where african  americans could live  attend school or own a business in the  valley  speaking of history black soldiers have  played a major role in our united states  armed forces  since the 1700s in the revolutionary war  all the way to present day  now while black service members are  visible they're still climbing the ranks  into lead roles  [Music]  during the pandemic our united states  air force thunderbirds flew across our  skies to honor frontline workers  energy is always high folks are excited  kids are screaming and yelling and  jumping up and down with excitement  as america's ambassadors in blue they've  got a secret weapon that keeps them  running smoothly  captain rimosha nelson this is something  that i've always wanted to be a part of  i was just kind of like blown away and  speechless but also like very excited  for the opportunity  to be a part of this team nelson a  howard university graduate  has lived across the world korea germany  turkey and now nella's air force base  coming from modest beginnings it was her  stepfather's stories of his time in the  navy  that left an impression i am so thankful  because he  really taught me the importance of  serving others nelson is the  thunderbird's first black female officer  when i found out i was extremely humbled  she's the team's public affairs officer  her role is selective nelson is the 12th  person to have the position  since the team started in 1953. she  leads an incredible team and as a leader  of that team they're the first people to  interface with the public  black soldiers have played a major role  in armed forces  from the buffalo soldiers tuskegee  airmen to the air force chief of staff  general charles brown for young black  girls to be able to see  themselves in me for them to get an  opportunity to see what's possible  is an absolute honor when we can inspire  folks to do that because they can relate  to us  that's very effective we can only do  that when we display diversity  and her vision for the future i hope  that we continue  to grow as a force and to be a  reflection of the people that we  actually serve  now earlier we mentioned the moulin  rouge and berkeley square  and you may be surprised to find out  that the most tangible pieces of that  history  are in the neon boneyard museum berkeley  square was designed by pioneering  architect paul williams  williams also designed the laconcho  motel and the sign now sits in the bone  yard  you'll also find the sign for the moulin  rouge along with some of the original  neon  that still works stay with us we've got  more ahead including a chance to meet  one of ccsd's most valuable teachers  the goal for me as an instructor is  always to empower students and ensure  that they have the  they don't see any limits to the  creativity it can bring to  to their lives we'll meet mr lang and  learn what it is he does that helps his  students realize their full potential  and the family of a former educator  realizes there's still a lot of work to  be done when it comes to reading level  equality  and they're working hard to close the  gap stay with us  like a mirror staring back at you and  really saw yourself  same hair same skin same dreams  [Music]  one clark county school district teacher  is making sure students  self-reflect he wants them to realize  their point of view  opinions and story matter that's right  well eight news now reporter sally  jaramillo spoke  to him and tell us more she tells us  more about his initiative  in empowering students lives being  someone who was pretty tech  forward before this i think definitely  lessened the  um i guess the complication of having to  do distance learning  michael lang has been a ccsd technology  instructor for 17 years  he is guiding his students at laura  deering elementary to change the world  the goal for me as an instructor is  always to empower students and ensure  that they have the  um they don't see any limits to the the  creativity it can bring to  uh to their lives one of the reasons why  i was always drawn to apple products is  the  the  the ease in which one can integrate  those products into your classroom and  allow students to really dream big  in 2020 lang along with fifth grade  students looked at the world and focused  on what was valuable to them  in a two and a half minute video  students voiced their opinions  then apple picked it up having people  look at my students as as role models as  people that could do things and i think  that was very empowering not only to  those fifth grade students but also  as an instructor to say okay cool i  perhaps am on the right path to get  these students doing what we need them  to do  lang says this fueled him to think  bigger this school year  and reached out to all grades i hate  the idea that we could only talk about  uh you know  the martin luther kings and the cesar  chavez is in the doors  and stuff during those months there's a  um  an importance that that life uh should  uh  she give you and that's an importance  you should live  with that power every day the first part  of lang's project is designed to show  students they are valuable by using the  children's book  you matter by christian robinson  students will also use  ipad to capture and edit images of  themselves  family neighbors and craft why they  matter  using the pbs kids scratch junior coding  app on  ipad we kind of came out of  the idea that a lot of what we do in  school is look at this other person  as this role model as this superhero as  whatever  and again this not this um really  imbuing this sense of self-confidence  this  self-esteem lang wants the students to  know they are important and deserve an  opportunity to show who they are  through self-portraits they will also  look at some very important people who  have paved the way  where we are now wasn't where we always  were finally  in the i'm a dreamer 2 segment lang will  ask them how they can be of service to  each other  he wants the students to understand the  story of las vegas  the story of nevada is bigger than the  things we see on a regular basis  we're not a perfect community but we're  a community that is  going through the process of being  perfected  reporting in las vegas well students  will complete interactive workbooks and  keynote for each part of the project  they will then compile their thoughts  into a collaborative book of their ideas  in which  they will have the opportunity to share  with the community organizers  and legislators how fantastic wow  well as we know small business owners  have been hit hard by the pandemic  felicia parkers is a black business  owner she is the co-founder of occasion  baskets  while striving to keep her business  going she is also giving back to the  community  parker says her biggest inspiration is  former first lady michelle obama  she says her goal is to bring joy to  others through her personalized baskets  moved by frontline workers she's shown  her gratitude by giving out more than  100 baskets to seven hospital ers  and her giving doesn't stop there we  give back every year we have a give back  um initiative a give back program that  we work on every year  and so what we try to do is give um to  organizations again that touch our  hearts personally  i'm a mother i'm a wife um and i'm a  minority  so a lot of those hit very close to home  for me  some of those organizations include the  school district project 150  hope for prisoners and the women's  resource center  well a las vegas family has launched an  effort to get young children  into reading it's all themed for black  history month and read by local people  of color about civil rights  love and diversity long before you took  your place in this world  you were dreamed of it's a line from the  book all because you matter that  barbershop owner jarrod essler is  reading to children online i have two  little kids myself you know  a six-year-old and a four-year-old and i  believe it's really important just to  read to them every night this is one of  25 special books written by someone of  color  and tells a story about a black or brown  person and you hear pop-ups  whispers it's part of a virtual reading  series for black history month called  we see you which was launched by the  parker family and their organization  saryubana culture it included themes  like civil rights  love and activism this project is  extremely intentional  in the way that we wanted our  the kids that were viewing these videos  to also  turn on the youtube video and to see  people who look like them  reading who have had an array of careers  we had a vision of a book sharing trisha  parker  a former teacher and her husband got the  idea after spotting an outdoor library  in la  and wanted to get more children  interested in reading 82 percent  of african-american children were  reading below proficient level  77 percent of latinx students we're  reading  below proficient level one day you'll be  big enough to reach it yourself  the couple decided to do a daily book  read online by authors of color and read  by las vegas residents  also of color that children could look  up to among the group  teachers business owners and other black  professionals like cj hayes  it's perfectly fine to ask for help so  to see  people of color in books and show that  to my daughter at a young age  it's going to really give her that  confidence as she grows and that's why i  think this is important  the books are targeting young children  and each clip is free the production  according to the parkers  is built on love and hope to get more  kids of color interested in the rich  culture  well new book reads are posted during  the week you can go online and have one  of 25 books read  free of charge visit 8newsnow.com for  the link  ahead on honoring black history who  doesn't love a home-cooked meal  we talked to grits cafe about how owners  felt the need to keep the soul alive  during the pandemic  [Music]  we all know food is comfort and during  these times we all need a little bit of  it  local business grits cafe says it will  continue to feed the community's soul  through these times sally had a meal  with the story  grits cafe is a community  based restaurant we've been in business  now for 14 years  grits cafe sets it's the ritz for the  grids  located near martha luther king and lake  mead boulevard  it offers southern familiarity to diners  we're all about economic empowerment so  when i first  originally opened grits cafe my idea was  to have a cafe where  people would come and do business and  they would feel like they were at home  in their mother's kitchen trina giles  owns grits cafe  while trying to stay afloat and adapt to  changes during the pandemic she also  continues to give back to the community  including first responders i believe  that it's important to make sure that we  provide  since we're being blessed with our loyal  customer base that we really do  provide some assistance to youth  organizations that's trying to make an  impact on our youth and  bring 